Cache _os_cpu_number: stop issuing a getcpu syscall on every malloc.

Profiling a guest process spawn under cider (strace -f -c of `cider shell true`) showed getcpu was
~90% of ALL syscalls and every call errored: ~24,000 failing getcpu per short-lived process. Cause:
libmalloc calls _malloc_cpu_number() on every allocation to pick a per-cpu magazine, which on DARLING
routed _os_cpu_number() straight to a getcpu Linux syscall (libsyscall/os/tsd.h). Real Darwin reads the
cpu number from a register (TPIDRRO_EL0 low bits / commpage), not a syscall; cider has no such fast
path, and TPIDRRO_EL0 is used here for the TSD base, so it fell back to the syscall on the hottest path
in the process.

The cpu number is only a magazine HINT, and on cider getcpu returns the same value for the process
(here it fails and yields 0 every time), so cache the first result and skip the syscall thereafter. This
turns ~24k getcpu/spawn into ~one per libmalloc translation unit. Per-TU static, benign int race; the
magazine hint becoming sticky-per-process is fine for the build-tool workload (largely single-threaded
per process, and per-cpu magazines were already inert since getcpu failed).
---
--- a/libsyscall/os/tsd.h
+++ b/libsyscall/os/tsd.h
@@ -63,16 +63,23 @@
 _os_cpu_number(void)
 {
 #ifdef DARLING
+	// Called on EVERY malloc (libmalloc magazine selection), so a getcpu syscall per call is a
+	// startup hot path: measured ~24k getcpu/short-lived process under cider. The value is only a
+	// per-cpu magazine HINT (real Darwin reads it from a register, not a syscall), so cache the first
+	// result and skip the syscall thereafter -- getcpu also currently fails here, yielding 0 anyway.
 	extern int __linux_syscall(int nr, ...);
+
+	static int _cpu_num_cache = -1;
+	int cached = _cpu_num_cache;
+	if (__builtin_expect(cached >= 0, 1)) {
+		return (unsigned int) cached;
+	}
 
 	unsigned int cpu_num = 0;
 	int status = __linux_syscall(__NR_getcpu, &cpu_num, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0);
-	// should we even check? i don't think it's possible for it to fail with these arguments
-	if (status < 0) {
-		return 0; // i guess?
-	}
-
-	return cpu_num;
+	cached = (status < 0) ? 0 : (int) cpu_num;
+	_cpu_num_cache = cached;
+	return (unsigned int) cached;
 #else // !DARLING
 #if defined(__arm__)
 	uintptr_t p;
